RustPythonJavaScriptC#KotlinJavaC <p>Import <code>Config</code> and <code>EnvironmentType</code> from <code>fatoora_core::config</code>.</p> Operation Returns Behavior <code>Config::new(env: EnvironmentType)</code> <code>Config</code> Stores the selected environment. <code>Config::default()</code> <code>Config</code> Selects <code>EnvironmentType::NonProduction</code>. <code>config.env()</code> <code>EnvironmentType</code> Returns the stored value. <code>EnvironmentType::from_str(value: &amp;str)</code> <code>Result&lt;EnvironmentType, EnvironmentParseError&gt;</code> Requires <code>std::str::FromStr</code> in scope. <code>env.as_str()</code> <code>&amp;'static str</code> Returns the canonical string below. <code>env.endpoint_url()</code> <code>&amp;'static str</code> Returns the base URL above. <p>The variants are <code>NonProduction</code>, <code>Simulation</code>, and <code>Production</code>. Parsing accepts <code>non_production</code>, <code>simulation</code>, and <code>production</code>, case-insensitively. It does not trim whitespace or accept <code>sandbox</code> as an alias. Canonical strings are lowercase.</p> <p>Import <code>Config</code> and <code>Environment</code> from <code>fatoora</code>.</p> Operation Returns Behavior <code>Config(env=Environment.NON_PRODUCTION)</code> <code>Config</code> Creates configuration; defaults to non-production. <code>config.env()</code> <code>Environment</code> Returns the stored value. <code>Environment(value)</code> <code>Environment</code> Converts integer <code>0</code>, <code>1</code>, or <code>2</code>; invalid values raise <code>ValueError</code>. <code>env.value</code> <code>int</code> Returns the numeric value. <p><code>Environment</code> is an integer enum: <code>NON_PRODUCTION = 0</code>, <code>SIMULATION = 1</code>, <code>PRODUCTION = 2</code>. It does not parse environment names. Configuration objects support <code>with</code> and <code>close()</code>; see Python resource handling.</p> <p>Import <code>Config</code> from <code>fatoority</code>.</p> Operation Returns Behavior <code>Config.new_(env)</code> <code>Config</code> Pass integer <code>0</code>, <code>1</code>, or <code>2</code>. <code>config.env()</code> <code>number</code> Returns the stored environment. <p>Use <code>0</code> for non-production, <code>1</code> for simulation, <code>2</code> for production. There is no implicit default. Invalid environments throw an <code>Error</code> with a <code>BindingError</code> cause. See JavaScript setup for loading the package.</p> <p>Use the <code>Fatoora</code> namespace.</p> Operation Returns Behavior <code>Config.New(byte env)</code> <code>Config</code> Pass <code>0</code>, <code>1</code>, or <code>2</code>. <code>config.Env()</code> <code>byte</code> Returns the stored environment. <p>Use <code>0</code> for non-production, <code>1</code> for simulation, <code>2</code> for production. There is no implicit default. Invalid environments throw <code>BindingErrorException</code>. Use <code>using</code> to dispose configuration when finished. See C# setup.</p> <p>Import <code>com.fatoora.Config</code>.</p> Operation Returns Behavior <code>Config.create(env: Int)</code> <code>Config</code> Pass <code>0</code>, <code>1</code>, or <code>2</code>. <code>config.env()</code> <code>Int</code> Returns the stored environment. <p>Use <code>0</code> for non-production, <code>1</code> for simulation, <code>2</code> for production. There is no implicit default. Invalid values raise an exception. Use <code>.use { ... }</code> or <code>close()</code> for configuration objects. See Kotlin setup.</p> <p>Import <code>com.fatoora.Config</code>.</p> Operation Returns Behavior <code>Config.create(int env)</code> <code>Config</code> Pass <code>0</code>, <code>1</code>, or <code>2</code>. <code>config.env()</code> <code>int</code> Returns the stored environment. <p>Use <code>0</code> for non-production, <code>1</code> for simulation, <code>2</code> for production. There is no implicit default. Invalid values raise an exception. Use try-with-resources or <code>close()</code> for configuration objects. See Java setup.</p> <p>Include <code>fatoora.h</code>. Pass <code>0</code> for non-production, <code>1</code> for simulation, or <code>2</code> for production. There is no implicit default; other values return an error.</p> <pre><code>FatooraStatus fatoora_config_new(int32_t env,
    FatooraConfig **out, FatooraError **error);
FatooraStatus fatoora_config_env(const FatooraConfig *self,
    uint8_t *out, FatooraError **error);
void fatoora_config_free(FatooraConfig *self);
</code></pre> <p>On <code>FATOORA_OK</code>, the constructor writes an owned configuration to <code>out</code> and the getter writes the environment number. Release configuration with <code>fatoora_config_free</code>. See C error and ownership conventions.</p>

RustPythonJavaScriptC#KotlinJavaC <p>Rust operations return errors for their module: <code>InvoiceError</code>, <code>CsrError</code>, <code>SigningError</code>, <code>QrCodeError</code>, <code>InvoiceXmlError</code>, <code>ParseError</code>, <code>XmlValidationError</code>, <code>ZatcaValidationError</code>, <code>ZatcaError</code>, <code>DecimalError</code>, and <code>EnvironmentParseError</code>. Each has a <code>kind()</code> method for shared classification.</p> <p>Convert a module error into <code>fatoora_core::Error</code> when combining operations. The conversion retains the original error, including validation issues and API responses. <code>Display</code> provides a human-readable message; <code>details_json()</code> provides the binding representation.</p> <pre><code>use fatoora_core::{Error, ErrorKind};
use fatoora_core::invoice::{InvoiceError, InvoiceBuilder};

fn build(builder: InvoiceBuilder) -&gt; Result&lt;(), Error&gt; {
    let _invoice = builder.build()?;
    Ok(())
}

fn inspect(error: &amp;Error) {
    if let Error::Invoice(InvoiceError::Validation(validation)) = error {
        for issue in validation.issues() {
            eprintln!("{:?}: {:?}", issue.field(), issue.kind());
        }
    }
    if error.kind() == ErrorKind::Validation {
        eprintln!("{}", error.details_json());
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Diagnostics and matching errors</p> <p>Backend failures are converted to <code>Diagnostic</code>, which exposes <code>message()</code>, <code>file()</code>, <code>line()</code>, <code>column()</code>, and <code>severity()</code>. Locations and severity are optional. Line and column numbers are one-based. Severity is <code>Warning</code>, <code>Error</code>, or <code>Fatal</code> when available. Property-file parsing preserves the reported line. Backend-specific numeric error codes are outside the public contract.</p> <p>Diagnostic wording can change. Use error kinds and structured fields for decisions.</p> <p>Public error enums are <code>#[non_exhaustive]</code>; downstream matches need a fallback arm. New variants can be added. Existing variant fields keep their types and meaning; private diagnostic fields can grow behind accessors.</p> <p>Native-operation exceptions inherit from <code>FfiError</code> and <code>FatooraError</code>. These exceptions carry <code>.code</code>, <code>.kind</code>, and <code>.details</code>. Unknown codes produce <code>FfiError</code>, with <code>.kind</code> set to <code>None</code>; the original code and details remain available.</p> <pre><code>from fatoora import InvoiceBuilder, InvoiceSubType, InvoiceTypeKind
from fatoora.errors import ValidationError

builder = InvoiceBuilder.new(InvoiceTypeKind.TAX, InvoiceSubType.SIMPLIFIED)
try:
    builder.build()
except ValidationError as error:
    for issue in error.details.get("issues", []):
        print(issue["field"], issue["kind"])
</code></pre> <p>Argument conversion and application code can also raise ordinary Python exceptions, such as <code>TypeError</code> or <code>ValueError</code>.</p> <p>Mapped exception classes are <code>InvalidInputError</code>, <code>ValidationError</code>, <code>ParseError</code>, <code>XmlError</code>, <code>CryptoError</code>, <code>IoError</code>, <code>NetworkError</code>, <code>UnauthorizedError</code>, <code>InternalError</code> and <code>ApiError</code>, in numeric code order. Local execution failures may expose a partial validation report at <code>error.details["report"]</code>.</p> <p>Native operations throw an <code>Error</code> whose <code>cause</code> is a <code>BindingError</code>. Inspect the cause for <code>code()</code>, <code>message()</code> and <code>detailsJson()</code>; parse the latter with <code>JSON.parse</code>. Transport and application errors can have other causes.</p> <pre><code>import { BindingError } from "fatoority";

function inspectError(error) {
  if (error?.cause instanceof BindingError) {
    const details = error.cause;
    console.error(details.code(), details.message());
    console.error(JSON.parse(details.detailsJson()));
  } else {
    console.error(error);
  }
}
</code></pre> <p>The error object uses garbage-collected cleanup. A local validation call returning JSON means a report was produced; inspect <code>is_valid</code> to decide whether the invoice passed.</p> <p>Native operations throw <code>BindingErrorException</code>. Its <code>Inner</code> owns a <code>BindingError</code>: read <code>Code()</code>, <code>Message()</code> and <code>DetailsJson()</code>, then dispose it. The exception's <code>Message</code> is copied text.</p> <pre><code>catch (BindingErrorException error)
{
    using var details = error.Inner;
    Console.Error.WriteLine($"{details.Code()}: {details.Message()}");
    Console.Error.WriteLine(details.DetailsJson());
}
</code></pre> <p>A returned local validation report still needs its <code>is_valid</code> field checked.</p> <p>Native operations throw <code>BindingException</code> with <code>code</code>, <code>message</code> and <code>detailsJson</code>. These are copied managed values; the exception needs no cleanup. Parse <code>detailsJson</code> with your application's JSON library. Calls on a closed owner throw <code>IllegalStateException</code>.</p> <p>A returned local validation report still needs its <code>is_valid</code> field checked.</p> <p>Native operations throw <code>BindingException</code>. Read <code>getCode()</code>, <code>getMessage()</code> and <code>getDetailsJson()</code>. These are copied managed values; the exception needs no cleanup. Calls on a closed owner throw <code>IllegalStateException</code>.</p> <p>A returned local validation report still needs its <code>is_valid</code> field checked.</p> <p>The C API uses opaque <code>FatooraError</code> owners and a shared <code>FatooraStatus</code>:</p> <pre><code>#include "fatoora.h"

FatooraStatus fatoora_error_code(const FatooraError *error);
const char *fatoora_error_message(const FatooraError *error);
const char *fatoora_error_details_json(const FatooraError *error);
void fatoora_error_free(FatooraError *error);
</code></pre> <p>Pass <code>FatooraError **error</code> as the last argument of an operation, or <code>NULL</code> when only the status matters. <code>FATOORA_OK</code> indicates success; other values preserve the numeric classification and structured native details. The error's message and JSON strings are borrowed until <code>fatoora_error_free()</code>. Output slots are cleared on entry: release a previous error before reusing its slot.</p> <p>C allocation failure can leave the error or its diagnostic strings unavailable; use the operation's returned status to decide success. Error accessors accept <code>NULL</code>: code returns <code>FATOORA_OK</code>, message returns an empty string, and details returns <code>{}</code>. Freeing <code>NULL</code> is safe. See C bindings for allocation and ownership rules.</p> <p>For local validation, <code>FATOORA_OK</code> means a report was returned; inspect its JSON <code>is_valid</code> field. A failure can include a partial report in the error details.</p>

